# Target C-II — the live adjacent switching forest has at most `1038` edges

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only source-level structural strengthening  
**Scope:** full-core Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.844`  
**Dependencies:** matched atomic gap `g=0.007707`; Section-171 residual envelope; `t<=1.844` retained dominant mass `z>2.2069`; audited adjacent-backbone switch budgets; adjacent Pareto refinement; adjacent switching-caterpillar theorem  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_adjacent_switch_count_1039.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The count-focused adjacent Pareto theorem previously used only the universal
Hopf--Pannwitz/Cauchy bound

\[
h_H\le q\le\pi D
\]

for the number `h_H` of actual diameter edges.  In the live `t<=1.844` branch the
current defect ledger is much tighter.  Combining it with the already-proved dominant
retained-mass floor reduces the finite switching obstruction from at most `1762` edges
to at most `1038` edges.

## Theorem SW1039

Every sufficiently large survivor in the proof-only full-core Type-II branch with

\[
t={q-k\over D}\le1.844
\]

has two consecutive smallest-leaf O--O cuts on the audited good-leaf backbone such
that their switching diameter graph satisfies

\[
\boxed{N_i\le1038.}
\tag{S39.1}

The same pair has

\[
B_i=O(D^{-1})
\]

for switching KKT multiplier mass and

\[
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(u_i,u_{i+1})=O(D^{-1})
\]

for projective phase mismatch.  Its switching graph is the layer cut of the full
diameter graph and hence, by the adjacent switching theorem, is a caterpillar forest.

Thus the large-first-order finite cusp has the normal form

```text
at most 1038 active switching diameter edges
+ disjoint union of caterpillars
+ exact layer-cut support
+ switching beta mass O(1/D)
+ adjacent phase mismatch O(1/D)
+ exact opposite two-cut multishear
```

for all sufficiently large members of the live branch.

---

## 1. The defect ledger bounds the full diameter-edge density

Write

\[
k=d_H+s_Z,
\qquad
\kappa={k\over D},
\]

where

\[
d_H=q-h_H.
\]

The exact identity

\[
q-k=h_H-s_Z
\]

gives

\[
\boxed{{h_H\over D}=t+{s_Z\over D}\le t+\kappa.}
\tag{S39.2}

Use the matched atomic constant

\[
C_{707}=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.007707,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\]

The Section-171 residual envelope is

\[
2\delta+{\kappa\over2}+h_{res}
=R_t:={9t\over2}-2\pi-C_{707},
\tag{S39.3}
\]

with

\[
h_{res}\ge0.
\]

The uniform `t<=1.844` phase theorem proves retained dominant outer-occurrence mass

\[
z>2.2069-o(1).
\]

Since

\[
F(z)=z-6\sin(z/6)
\]

is increasing and selected dominant occurrence energy is part of the outer detour,

\[
\boxed{
\delta>F(2.2069)-o(1).
}
\tag{S39.4}

The exact certificate gives

\[
F(2.2069)>0.0494260581341351.
\tag{S39.5}

At the adverse endpoint `t=1.844`, substitute (S39.4) into (S39.3):

\[
{\kappa\over2}
\le R_{1.844}-2F(2.2069)+o(1).
\]

Using outward Machin/radical/Taylor enclosures, the certificate obtains

\[
\boxed{
\kappa<0.007259926+o(1).
}
\tag{S39.6}

Therefore (S39.2) gives

\[
\boxed{
{h_H\over D}<1.851259926+o(1).
}
\tag{S39.7}

This is the key improvement over the old `h_H/D<=pi` input.

---

## 2. The global switching-count budget drops to `3.70252D`

For the selected consecutive good-leaf path, each actual diameter interval starts and
stops at most twice.  Hence the parent interval theorem gives

\[
\sum_i N_i\le2h_H.
\]

Equation (S39.7) therefore yields

\[
\boxed{
\sum_iN_i
<3.702519852D+o(D).
}
\tag{S39.8}

The audited path has

\[
m>pD-o(D),
\qquad
p={278778993\over78216868750}
=0.0035641799199\ldots .
\]

Thus the count-only average threshold is

\[
{3.702519852\over p}
=1038.81396\ldots .
\tag{S39.9}

So the integer `1039` lies strictly above the switch-count average.

---

## 3. Keep phase and KKT switching mass small on the same boundary

The parent adjacent theorem independently gives

\[
\sum_i D B_i\le2D
\tag{S39.10}
\]

and

\[
\sum_iD(q_i+q_{i+1})
\le{2C_{phase}\over d_{low}}D+o(D),
\tag{S39.11}
\]

with

\[
d_{low}={9891\over62500}.
\]

Use the Pareto union-bound argument with thresholds

\[
n=1039,
\qquad
a=b=10^{11}.
\]

The exact verifier bounds the three bad-boundary densities by

\[
0.00356354172406\ldots,
\]

\[
2\times10^{-11},
\]

and

\[
6.7191544\times10^{-10},
\]

respectively.  Their sum is below `p` by more than

\[
6.3\times10^{-7}.
\]

Hence one boundary is simultaneously good for all three conditions:

\[
N_i<1039+o(1),
\]

\[
DB_i<10^{11}+o(1),
\]

\[
D(q_i+q_{i+1})<10^{11}+o(1).
\]

Since `N_i` is integer, sufficiently far along the survivor sequence

\[
N_i\le1038.
\]

The two large fixed constants still give `B_i=O(D^{-1})` and phase gap
`O(D^{-1})`, exactly the asymptotic scales needed by the finite-cusp theorem.

---

## 4. Structural consequence

The switching rows are exactly

\[
\delta_H(S_{i+1}\setminus S_i),
\]

a bipartite layer cut of the straight-line diameter thrackle.  The adjacent
switching-caterpillar theorem therefore upgrades (S39.1) to

\[
\boxed{
H_{sw}\text{ is a caterpillar forest with at most }1038\text{ edges}
}
\tag{S39.12}

for every sufficiently large survivor.

This does not remove the full-support KKT max-function cusp; local old-contact O--O
shear descent remains blocked.  It does make the remaining interface obstruction
substantially smaller and more rigid than the original `<21200` arbitrary-row normal
form.

A next closing theorem should use the cyclic endpoint order of a diameter caterpillar,
together with the two adjacent cut-resultant equations, to constrain the signs of these
at most `1038` exceptional rows or replace the layer nonlocally.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only strengthening of the adjacent finite-cusp normal
form. C-II and Level I remain open.
